insert into public.day_recaps (series_id, season_number, week, day, recap) values
('dark-moon', 1, 3, 1, 'Corin remembered the girl who was held with him. Her name was Wren, her home was always warm, and they told her she was going home first. The ledger and Meira''s list both named her village, Ashcombe, on the edge of the burned country, a long day west. Before dawn one of them came up out of the hill alone, fought you without a word and fled, and below the shaft a cart and a child''s small prints turned west. You carried Corin home to the mill, and he told his mother every one of your names. Why would anyone take a stolen child back to where she was born?'),
('dark-moon', 1, 3, 2, 'In Ashcombe, Wren''s mother told you that two polite men in grey coats had taken her two weeks ago. They asked for her by name, and they knew the night she was born, a night the moon went out. One of them said they would only need her the once, and he looked her in the eye when he said it. The night before you came, a cart with no lamps carried Wren past her own door and up the black road. Garrick knew that road. “My family''s been in these hills longer than the roads have.” Half a day up it lies the Blackglass, the place Meira marked. What does anyone need a child for, once?'),
('dark-moon', 1, 3, 3, 'You crossed the Blackglass, a hillside where the rock once ran like wax, and split a boot on its far edge. Beside a shape you knew, an old stone housing opened at your touch. Tamsin, Corin and Wren were each taken from beside a place Meira marked, and you wrote it into her notebook: three children, three marks, one list. That evening you found her note in the margin beside the first fragment''s “Five fell”: “Five, the fragment says. Every other record I can find says four. I cannot find a fifth anywhere, and I have looked.” By morning Garrick had mended your boot, and the riders had passed the mark without slowing. What are they riding to, out in the black ridges?'),
('dark-moon', 1, 3, 4, 'In the black ridges you found a chip of black glass in a hoof-scratch, and another fifty paces on. Wren is dropping them, and the men who have her have not noticed. A hundred yards off the riders'' line, a buried doorway opened on a room cut from the ridge, where whoever wrote your fragments once slept. A bone die lay in one of its niches, and a fragment waited in a housing between them. At dusk you saw smoke from a hut to the north-east, and that night your own fire in the old doorway showed just as plainly. She is alive, and she is thinking. The hut, at first light.'),
('dark-moon', 1, 3, 5, 'Five more of Wren''s chips led you down to a shepherd''s hut beside a spring. A grey coat left on watch came down at you, then fled over bare rock that takes no prints. The hut was empty. WREN was scratched fresh into the doorframe at a child''s height, and cut long ago into the beam over her bed were the Order''s words, *Until the throne rise whole.* They had been there within the hour and left at a run, because from that doorway they could see your fire. “They''re always a step ahead of us,” Garrick said. Now it is a step and no more. The cairn on the highest ridge, before the light goes.'),
('dark-moon', 1, 3, 6, 'You raced the light to the cairn on the highest ridge of the burned country, and found the sixth fragment wedged at its foot. The six made a map once more. It ended at a round black hill with a crack across its crown, which Garrick said the shepherds call the Kiln. The way was said aloud, twice, for the ones on the slope below: in through the crack, and down a way cut long ago to a room beneath the hill. Not an hour after full dark, a single lamp crossed the black ground, climbed the Kiln, and went in. Whatever happens under that hill has started. Into the Kiln, tonight.'),
('dark-moon', 1, 3, 7, 'You went down through the crack in the Kiln''s crown into the warm dark, where a girl was crying, and glimpsed a robed figure climbing away up a vent with a small box held to his chest. You brought Wren down alive from a slope of loose black glass, her palm already cut and bound in white linen. Behind the stone table was the same swept, empty hollow, and the rubbing fitted it. The captive''s sealed stone case sprang open at your touch. “Seeker blood,” he whispered. His scripture said the king never lied to his people, only kept silent about the price of his gift, and that the Seekers who wrote your fragments, his own soldiers, struck him down. He did not die. He sleeps where he fell, and the Seekers locked their ways with their own blood. “Your blood. Their blood. The same.” Their book says the faithful shall not lie, so his answers stood: the children are taken to wake the king, and the fourth child is already with them. The Order is not mourning a dead king. It is waking a sleeping one. “We''re not finished with you,” the captive said, and every lamp went out. When one was lit again, his ropes lay cut clean.')
